# 0.3.15's reference arms on the one RTX 5090 32 GB: llama.cpp (mainline, or the model's pull-request tree) at the same flags
# family the bench page's rows used, one call a model through the family's depth runner (tools/ref/depth-qwen3moe.sh,
# depth-ds41.sh, depth-glm5next.sh), 2 rounds. The one term with a derivation is Qwen3.8's mainline decode at depth 512 with 39
# leading layers' experts on the host (-ncmoe 39, derived from the card's 31.7 GiB free: the A6000's 22 card layers hold 26 of
# 48, this card holds 9): the A6000 row of the bench page (44.68 tok/s at tg96, -ncmoe 26) scaled by the host layers, 26/39,
# is 29.8, and the card's larger share of the step reads above it, so 25..38 [derived, not measured]. The other models' arms
# are read beside it with no band of their own.
kind: baseline
question: what decode rate does mainline llama.cpp reach on Qwen3.8-Flash-Next UD-Q4_K_XL at depth 512 on the one RTX 5090 at -ncmoe 39?
term: tg tok/s @ n=96, depth 512, llama-bench -ngl 99 -fa on -lzm off -ncmoe 39 -t 32, mainline 53ed051ce built for sm_120a, RTX 5090 32 GB
unit: tok/s
predict: 25..38
condition: one lease a runner call, the 5090 alone (local mode, no other compute process on it), MemTotal ~251 GiB before the first call; BLOOMERY_AB_ROUNDS=2, the arms lcpp:512 lcpp:4096 lcpppp:512 lcpppp:4096 lcpppp4096:4096
reason: the bench page's A6000 row of the same arm and file, scaled by the host layers; the 5090's own rows are the table's reference column.
decide-in: the bench page takes the measured means with their rounds, beside ours from the sitting
decide-below: under 25: read the row's load lines (the layers on the card, a flag the tree ignored) and the card's memory before the row is published
decide-above: beyond 38: read the layers the tree put on the card against -ncmoe 39, and the witness blocks, before crediting the card
